OpenCvSharp native runtimes — FFmpeg
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

OpenCvSharp4.runtime.win and OpenCvSharp4.runtime.osx.arm64: the wrapper
package itself declares Apache-2.0 (verified via .nuspec, both
4.13.0.20260627). Both bundle statically-linked native third-party code
beyond OpenCV itself, confirmed by extracting build-configuration strings
and copyright strings from the shipped binaries themselves
(`strings runtimes/*/native/*`), since neither nupkg bundles a
LICENSE/COPYING/NOTICE file for any of this — only README.runtime.md:

  - FFmpeg. Statically linked into libOpenCvSharpExtern.dylib on osx-arm64
    (per README.runtime.md: "macOS ... packages include FFmpeg ...
    statically linked"); shipped as a separate, dynamically loaded
    opencv_videoio_ffmpeg4130_64.dll on win-x64. Embedded configure strings
    show NEITHER --enable-gpl NOR --enable-nonfree on either platform, and
    the osx-arm64 build explicitly --disable-libx264 --disable-libx265 (the
    two codecs that force FFmpeg to GPL); win-x64 only enables
    --enable-libopenh264 --enable-libvpx --enable-libaom, all permissively
    (BSD-style) licensed. That is consistent with this same package
    family's own README.runtime.md statement for its Linux variant — "the
    full (non-slim) package includes FFmpeg (LGPL v2.1) ... statically
    linked" — and is the basis for calling FFmpeg here LGPL-2.1+ rather
    than GPL. UNDETERMINED WITH CERTAINTY: no FFmpeg LICENSE.md/COPYING
    text is bundled in either nupkg, so this rests on binary-string
    inspection plus the vendor's parallel statement, not on a first-party
    licence file — flagged rather than smoothed over, per PLAN risk 7.
    Static linking under LGPL-2.1 also carries a relinking obligation (§6)
    that this notice does not yet resolve. CONCLUSION: not GPL-configured
    on either shipped platform — compatible with GPLv3, no STOP condition
    triggered.
  - libjpeg-turbo (IJG-style/BSD, "Copyright (C) 1991-2025 The
    libjpeg-turbo Project and many others") and libpng (the libpng/zlib
    licence, "Copyright (c) 1998-2002,2004,2006-2018 Glenn
    Randers-Pehrson" et al.) are also statically linked, per copyright
    strings found the same way. Both are permissive and do not affect
    GPLv3 compatibility.

Chase the LGPL-2.1 relinking obligation and a citable FFmpeg licence text
before a wide release; nothing above blocks GPLv3 as currently built.


SkiaSharp / HarfBuzzSharp native win32 binaries — further bundled components
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Win32 native assets (SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Win32,
HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.Win32) statically link a further ~20 third-party
components (ANGLE, HarfBuzz itself, Skia's own third_party libraries —
FreeType, ICU, libpng, libjpeg-turbo, libwebp, zlib, expat, and others),
enumerated with their individual licence texts in a THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt
file bundled inside HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.Win32 8.3.1.3 itself (found
at harfbuzzsharp.nativeassets.win32/8.3.1.3/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt in the
NuGet cache — 2716 lines, not reproduced here; treat that path as the exact
pointer this notice requires). Every licence family present there (MIT,
BSD, Apache-2.0, MPL, LGPL) is either permissive or upgrades cleanly into
GPLv3; none is GPL-only. Not independently verified: which of those ~20
components are actually linked into the specific win-x64 binary shipped
here versus present only in the Linux/macOS/WebAssembly variants covered by
the same combined notices file — chase before a wide release, same caveat
as the OpenCvSharp FFmpeg finding above.

The Microsoft.Extensions.* packages listed above each bundle their own
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.TXT (the standard dotnet/runtime one — e.g.
microsoft.extensions.configuration/10.0.12/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.TXT, 1418
lines), listing small embedded components (ASP.NET, Slicing-by-8 CRC, etc.)
under permissive licences (Apache-2.0, BSD). Not reproduced here for the
same reason; pointer given.


AvaloniaUI.DiagnosticsSupport
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

AvaloniaUI.DiagnosticsSupport (2.2.3) declares NO licence at all — its
.nuspec has no <license>/<licenseUrl> element, and the package contains no
LICENSE/COPYING file (only README.md, an icon, and build props/targets),
confirmed by inspecting both directly in the NuGet cache. This is not an
oversight in this notices file; the package itself ships unlicensed.

RESOLVED at I5: src/LoreFetch.App/App.csproj now references it as
  <PackageReference Include="AvaloniaUI.DiagnosticsSupport"
                     Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'" />
so a Release build — what `dotnet publish -r win-x64 --self-contained` for
the shipped binary actually is — never pulls it in, and the unlicensed
package never reaches a distributed artifact. It is listed here only
because it is still a PackageReference in a tracked .csproj (the hook's
advisory grep does not look at the Condition attribute), and because a
Debug build run locally still carries it — which is fine for development,
never for anything redistributed.


